"""Focused expansions after direct review of all eight lectures and assigned chapters."""
import json
from pathlib import Path

def enrich(D):
 Q=D['questions']
 def lesson(section,title,prompt,plain,answer,example,correct,wrong,refs,scenario):
  number=len(Q)+1
  item=dict(id=f'425-q{number:02}',number=number,section=section,title=title,q=prompt,teach=plain,answer=answer,example=example,correct=correct,distractors=wrong.split('|'),refs=refs,slides=[])
  Q.append(item)
  # A direct check and a different application check require distinct reasoning.
  for suffix,question,right,alternatives,explanation in [('recognize',prompt,correct,wrong,plain),('scenario',*scenario)]:
   D['checks'].append(dict(id=f'425-deep-{number}-{suffix}',source=number,section=section,q=question,correct=right,distractors=alternatives.split('|'),teach=explanation,answer=explanation,example=example,format='mc'))
  return number

 lesson(1,'Why attacks succeed','How do vulnerable software, configuration, resource limits, and business processes create different weaknesses?',
 'A flaw can exist in the product, in its settings, in its limited capacity, or in the way people do business. Fixing one category does not automatically fix another.',
 'Chapter 1 groups weaknesses into widespread vulnerabilities, configuration issues, poor software design, hardware limitations, and enterprise issues. Unsupported or end-of-life products may lack patches. Default settings and excessive account privileges are configuration issues. Unsafe input, error handling, and races are design issues. Resource exhaustion consumes CPU, memory, storage, or connections. Business-process compromise manipulates legitimate workflows; undocumented assets create system sprawl. Defenses include inventory, supported software, hardened settings, safe design, capacity controls, and verification of unusual business requests.',
 'A patched accounting system still processes a fraudulent payment because staff skip the callback check. This is a process weakness even though the software is up to date.',
 'Different weakness categories require different controls','Patching fixes every process weakness|All successful attacks require a zero-day|More bandwidth fixes excessive user privileges',[[3,'Ch. 1, pp. 12–17']],
 ('A new server was installed without informing IT, so it receives no updates. What is the most direct problem?','An undocumented asset contributing to system sprawl','A hash collision|A symmetric-key mismatch|A TCP acknowledgment delay','Without an inventory, the organization may not know the server needs ownership, monitoring, and updates.'))
 lesson(1,'Asset threat vulnerability and vector','How do asset, threat, threat actor, vulnerability, attack vector, attack surface, and risk connect?',
 'Use one story: something valuable is exposed through a weakness, an actor uses a route to exploit it, and the organization faces potential harm.',
 'An asset is something valuable. A threat is potential harm; a threat actor can cause that harm. A vulnerability is a weakness. An attack vector is a route or means of attack; the attack surface includes the collection of exposed opportunities. Risk concerns potential loss in context, including likelihood and impact. A threat is not proof an incident occurred. Reducing unnecessary services can shrink attack surface, while fixing a flaw removes a particular vulnerability.',
 'A student database is the asset, data theft is the threat, a criminal is the actor, an unpatched web application is the weakness, and the exposed application is an attack route.',
 'Asset is value; vulnerability is weakness; vector is route','Asset and threat always mean the same thing|An attack vector is the attacker’s salary|Every threat is already a successful breach',[[3,'Ch. 1, pp. 21–23']],
 ('A company removes three unused Internet-facing services. What has it most directly reduced?','Its exposed attack surface','The length of every hash digest|Its need for an asset inventory|The existence of every threat actor','Fewer exposed services mean fewer opportunities through which an attacker can reach the system.'))
 lesson(1,'Risk response choices','How can you distinguish accepting, avoiding, transferring, and mitigating risk?',
 'Ask what the organization actually does: acknowledges the risk, stops the activity, shares financial loss, or reduces exposure.',
 'Accepting risk acknowledges exposure under a deliberate decision. Avoidance stops the risky activity. Transfer shifts some financial consequences through insurance or an appropriate agreement; it does not remove all responsibility. Mitigation reduces likelihood or impact through controls. Deterrence discourages attackers. A course expression such as threat × vulnerability × cost is a conceptual aid, not a measured probability formula unless its variables are defined.',
 'For a risky remote service, canceling it is avoidance; adding MFA is mitigation; buying suitable insurance transfers some loss; documenting a tolerable residual exposure is acceptance.',
 'Choose the response according to the action taken','Buying insurance patches software|Accept means the risk was eliminated|Mitigate means never using the service',[[3,'Ch. 1, p. 23'],[13,'risk discussion']],
 ('A company continues a service but adds access restrictions and monitoring. Which response is this?','Mitigation','Avoidance|Transfer only|Ignoring risk','The activity continues with controls intended to lower likelihood or impact.'))
 lesson(1,'People products and procedures','Why can a security product fail without people and procedures?',
 'A tool needs someone to configure and use it, and a repeatable procedure for acting on what it finds.',
 'The textbook’s layers are products, people, and policies/procedures. Products include technical and physical safeguards. People configure and use them. Procedures define approved actions and escalation. Technical controls use technology, administrative controls guide behavior, and physical controls restrict physical access. Security must support useful business work; inconvenient controls may cause workarounds. Compliance is one requirement, not proof that attacks cannot succeed.',
 'A sensor produces alerts, but no one owns the review queue. Buying another sensor does not solve the missing monitoring responsibility.',
 'Tools, responsible people, and defined procedures must work together','A product automatically creates an incident process|Only IT staff affect security|Every compliant system is immune to attack',[[3,'Ch. 1, pp. 18–21, 33–35'],[13,'architecture and business priorities']],
 ('A written rule requires independent verification before changing supplier bank details. What kind of control is this?','An administrative procedure','A packet checksum|An asymmetric cipher|A hardware-only control','The rule governs how staff perform a business process. Technical enforcement may support it.'))
 lesson(1,'Threat actor attributes','How do capability, resources, access, and motivation change threat assessment?',
 'The actor’s reason and ability matter together. A person with little technical skill can still be dangerous if they have trusted access or easy-to-use tools.',
 'Compare sophistication, funding/resources, internal versus external position, and intent. Script kiddies use existing tools; hacktivists pursue ideological goals; criminals pursue gain; competitors may steal valuable research; brokers sell vulnerability knowledge; nation-state actors may conduct sustained espionage. APT describes sustained targeted activity, often associated with state actors. Insiders include employees, contractors, and partners. Assess behaviors and evidence rather than assuming nationality or a single label proves intent.',
 'An insider can export customer records through ordinary access, while a well-funded external group can patiently target a specific research system.',
 'Evaluate motivation and capability together with access','All insiders have malicious intent|Only experts can use attack tools|A zero-day is a category of employee',[[3,'Ch. 1, pp. 28–31'],[12,'motives and target examples']],
 ('An actor sells an undisclosed software weakness to another party. Which textbook category best fits?','Vulnerability broker','Security technician|Ordinary customer|Network hypervisor','The defining behavior is selling vulnerability knowledge, not necessarily executing the attack personally.'))
 lesson(1,'Roles and fundamental principles','How do security roles differ, and how do the five principles guide their decisions?',
 'Role questions match duties. Principle questions match the reason a control helps.',
 'In this textbook the CISO leads security, the manager supervises staff, the administrator manages daily technology and analyzes/designs solutions, and the technician configures and troubleshoots. Layering adds barriers; limiting grants necessary rights; diversity reduces common failure modes; obscurity conceals unnecessary details; simplicity keeps defenses manageable. Vendor diversity alone does not guarantee independence, and secrecy alone does not establish security.',
 'An administrator chooses a combination of access rules and endpoint controls. A technician installs and troubleshoots them. A manager allocates staffing for monitoring.',
 'Match role duties and distinguish the purpose of each principle','A technician and CISO always have identical duties|Using two identical rules guarantees diversity|Complexity always improves security',[[3,'Ch. 1, pp. 5–6, 32–35']],
 ('A team uses multiple barriers but the same product flaw defeats all of them. Which principle needs more attention?','Diversity','Ciphertext length|Steganography|Risk avoidance','Layer count is not enough when the layers share the same failure mode.'))
 lesson(1,'Why organizations protect information','What business consequences make information security necessary?',
 'Think beyond stolen files: a breach can impersonate customers, interrupt work, damage trust, and create reporting or contractual obligations.',
 'Chapter 1 discusses data theft, identity theft, legal/compliance consequences, lost productivity, and cyberterrorism affecting critical services. For course recognition, HIPAA relates to protected health information, Sarbanes-Oxley to public-company financial reporting/internal controls, GLBA to financial-information privacy, and PCI DSS to payment-card security as an industry standard. These are category associations from the assigned text, not current legal advice. Do not memorize the old edition’s fines or notification deadlines as current facts.',
 'A stolen patient record affects privacy and trust. A service outage diverts staff from normal work. A company may also need to investigate obligations with appropriate specialists.',
 'Security protects business continuity, information, identity, and trust','Only the replacement cost of hardware matters|PCI DSS is the same as a criminal statute|An unavailable system cannot cause business loss',[[3,'Ch. 1, pp. 24–27']],
 ('An incident forces staff to stop customer work for recovery. Which consequence is directly illustrated?','Lost productivity','Improved availability|Reduced attack surface automatically|Successful risk avoidance','Recovery consumes working hours and interrupts the business service even before other losses are calculated.'))
 lesson(7,'Data at rest in transit and in use','How does the state of data change the protection it needs?',
 'Stored data, moving data, and data being processed face different exposure points. Protecting one state does not protect all three.',
 'Data at rest is stored, such as a powered-off laptop’s files. Data in transit travels across a network. Data in use is being processed on an endpoint. Disk encryption helps against offline access; TLS protects a connection; endpoint controls and careful access restrictions protect use. Keys, recovery, trusted devices, and authentication matter in all cases. A keylogger exposes why encrypted transport cannot rescue plaintext already captured on the endpoint.',
 'A payroll file on disk, the same file being uploaded, and its contents open in a running application illustrate the three states.',
 'Stored, transmitted, and processed data need protection at their exposure points','TLS alone protects a stolen offline drive|Disk encryption prevents every keylogger|Data in use means only data crossing a router',[[3,'Ch. 3, p. 107'],[18,'data states'],[7,'keylogger report']],
 ('A thief removes a powered-off laptop’s drive and tries to read it elsewhere. Which control most directly addresses this exposure?','Appropriate disk encryption with protected keys','TCP retransmission|A browser reputation score|An email spam filter','The attack concerns offline access to stored data, rather than transmission reliability.'))
 lesson(7,'Substitution ROT13 and XOR','What do the simple ciphers teach, and what are their limits?',
 'A substitution replaces symbols. ROT13 shifts letters by 13 positions. XOR compares bits: same gives 0 and different gives 1.',
 'ROT13 applied twice recovers the original letters, demonstrating reversibility. XOR has the useful identity (message XOR key) XOR key = message. For example 1010 XOR 0110 = 1100, then 1100 XOR 0110 = 1010. These operations teach algorithms and keys. ROT13 has no meaningful secrecy against someone who knows the rule; repeating a short XOR key is not a secure replacement for a designed cryptographic scheme.',
 'Using the same XOR bit pattern twice cancels it. This is different from hashing, where there is no corresponding decryption step.',
 'XOR returns 0 for equal bits and 1 for different bits','1 XOR 1 equals 2|ROT13 is a one-way hash|XOR always gives 1',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 103–104'],[18,'simple cipher examples']],
 ('What is 1010 XOR 0110?','1100','1110|1000|0011','Compare each pair: 1/0 gives 1, 0/1 gives 1, 1/1 gives 0, and 0/0 gives 0.'))
 lesson(7,'Randomness confusion and diffusion','Why do cryptographic randomness, confusion, and diffusion matter?',
 'A secret key must be hard to predict. The output should not expose simple patterns linking the message and key.',
 'Cryptographic randomness supplies unpredictable secrets. A PRNG is algorithmic and deterministic; a cryptographically secure generator must be designed and seeded appropriately. Diffusion spreads a plaintext change through output. Confusion complicates the relation between key and ciphertext. These frustrate pattern analysis, but do not excuse weak keys, implementation errors, or exposed secrets. An ordinary predictable random-number generator is not suitable simply because its output looks irregular.',
 'If every key is derived from the current minute, a strong cipher may still be easy to guess. Strong mathematics cannot compensate for predictable key generation.',
 'Unpredictable secrets and hidden input/key patterns resist analysis','A hidden algorithm removes the need for keys|More randomness changes hash output length|Diffusion means packets arrive faster',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 104–105']],
 ('Changing one plaintext bit changes many ciphertext bits. Which property does this illustrate?','Diffusion','Risk transfer|Availability|A DNS zone transfer','The effect of the input change is spread across the output rather than confined to one matching position.'))
 lesson(7,'Stream block sponge and one-time pad','How do processing structure and key use distinguish these constructions?',
 'These names describe different constructions. Do not infer security just from the name or assume every variable-length construction is an encryption cipher.',
 'A stream cipher combines data with a keystream; a block cipher transforms fixed-size blocks under a key. Secure modes determine how blocks form a message. Sponge constructions absorb input and generate output; SHA-3 uses this family of designs, while extendable-output variants can request different lengths. SHA-256 itself has a fixed-length digest. A true one-time pad requires a uniformly random secret key as long as the message, used once only. Reusing it destroys its special security. Stream ciphers are not inherently weaker than block ciphers.',
 'A short password repeated across a long file is not a one-time pad even if the program uses XOR.',
 'Structure and correct key use both matter; a true one-time pad is never reused','All stream ciphers are always insecure|SHA-256 produces any length requested|Any repeated password is a one-time pad',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 109–110'],[18,'algorithm categories']],
 ('A supposed one-time pad uses the same secret pad for two messages. Which requirement was violated?','Use the random pad only once','Use a shorter pad than the message|Publish the pad for verification|Generate a variable-length checksum','The security argument depends on an independent, secret, unreused random pad.'))
 lesson(7,'Hashes HMAC and trusted comparisons','What can a hash comparison establish, and what does HMAC add?',
 'A plain hash is a fingerprint anyone can calculate. HMAC adds a secret shared by the parties, so unauthorized changes are harder to forge.',
 'MD5, SHA families, and RIPEMD are hash families. SHA-256 outputs 256 bits, shown by 64 hexadecimal characters. MD5 and SHA-1 have broken collision resistance. HMAC uses a hash and a secret key for message authentication/integrity; it does not encrypt. Both holders of a shared HMAC key can produce a tag, so it is not a publicly verifiable proof of which holder acted. For download checking, the expected digest must come through a trusted channel. An attacker replacing a file and its untrusted posted hash defeats simple comparison.',
 'An analyst checks a downloaded file against an independently authenticated expected digest. A plain hash copied from the attacker’s page would not give the same assurance.',
 'A trusted digest detects changes; HMAC adds shared-secret authentication','HMAC encrypts and hides the whole message|An untrusted posted hash proves authenticity|MD5 has guaranteed unique outputs',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 110–113'],[18,'hash properties and families']],
 ('Two people share an HMAC key. Can one use their matching tag as public proof that only the other created the message?','No; either key holder can create the tag','Yes, because HMAC uses a secret|Yes, because a hash has no collisions|Yes, because the message was encrypted','Shared-key authentication differs from a signature checked with a public verification key.'))
 lesson(7,'Symmetric algorithm recognition','How do DES, 3DES, AES, RC4, Blowfish, Twofish, and IDEA fit the categories?',
 'First classify the mechanism. A name ending in 256 may describe a key or digest length depending on the algorithm.',
 'DES is a symmetric block cipher with a 56-bit key. 3DES applies the DES primitive three times; the two are historical course examples. AES is a symmetric block cipher with 128-bit blocks and keys of 128, 192, or 256 bits. AES total rounds are 10, 12, and 14 respectively; the textbook’s 9/11/13 count refers to its repeated middle-stage description, not the total standard rounds. RC4 is a historical stream cipher. Blowfish and Twofish are block ciphers; IDEA is also symmetric. Recognition is the study goal, not deploying every algorithm named in an old book.',
 'AES-256 uses a 256-bit key but still transforms 128-bit blocks. SHA-256 instead names a 256-bit hash digest.',
 'AES is symmetric with 128-bit blocks; its suffix specifies key length','AES-256 always uses 256-bit blocks|RSA is a symmetric stream cipher|SHA-256 is AES with a different key',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 114–116'],[18,'DES and algorithm families']],
 ('Which statement about AES-256 is correct?','Its key is 256 bits and its block size is 128 bits','Its block size and key are both 256 bits|It produces a 256-bit hash digest|It uses a public/private key pair','Key size, block size, and hash-output size describe different quantities.'))
 lesson(7,'Key distribution and hybrid encryption','Why combine symmetric and asymmetric cryptography?',
 'Symmetric encryption is efficient, but both parties need the secret. Public-key methods help establish or protect that secret without sharing a private key.',
 'In hybrid encryption, a fresh symmetric session key protects bulk data. A suitable public-key scheme protects that session key for the recipient, or an authenticated key agreement establishes it. The receiver uses the appropriate private-key capability to recover or derive the session secret, then decrypts the bulk data. PGP is the textbook example. Public keys still need trusted identity binding. The sender’s private key is not sent to the receiver, and public-key encryption alone does not authenticate who sent the message.',
 'A file is encrypted under a random session key. Only the intended recipient can recover that key from the protected key material and open the file.',
 'Use efficient symmetric bulk protection with authenticated public-key establishment','Send the recipient your private key|Encrypt every byte with an untrusted posted public key|Hash the file and call it encrypted',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 116–117, 127']],
 ('PGP protects a large message symmetrically and protects its session key for Bob. Whose private key recovers the protected session key?','Bob’s private key','Alice’s public key|Every recipient’s shared signing key|The mail server’s checksum','Bob is the intended recipient. His private capability complements the public key used to protect the session key.'))
 lesson(7,'RSA ECC signatures and key exchange','What distinct jobs do RSA, ECC, signatures, and Diffie-Hellman perform?',
 'Asymmetric cryptography is a family, not one operation. Encryption, signing, and agreement of a session secret have different goals.',
 'RSA uses arithmetic related to large primes and supports suitable encryption/signature schemes. ECC is a family using elliptic-curve mathematics and can support signatures or agreement with different constructions. DSA is a signature algorithm, not bulk-data encryption. Diffie-Hellman establishes a shared secret; it does not by itself authenticate who participated. Ephemeral DH/ECDH can support forward secrecy so later long-term key compromise does not reveal properly established past sessions. Do not infer that every use of DH is static or every public-key algorithm has forward secrecy.',
 'A secure protocol authenticates the parties and establishes a fresh session secret, then uses efficient symmetric encryption for the conversation.',
 'Select encryption, signing, or authenticated key agreement according to the goal','Diffie-Hellman alone proves civil identity|DSA is a disk-encryption mode|All RSA use automatically provides forward secrecy',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 118–123'],[18,'asymmetric algorithms']],
 ('Two parties agree on a secret using unauthenticated DH. What remains unresolved?','Whether they agreed with the intended party','Whether both could calculate a shared value|Whether IP can route packets|Whether block ciphers exist','Agreement needs authentication to avoid an active intermediary substituting their own exchange.'))
 lesson(7,'Signature verification from start to finish','What does a recipient actually check when verifying a signed message?',
 'The signature must match both the message and the trusted sender key. Verifying a signature is different from decrypting a message for privacy.',
 'The sender signs a representation of the message using a private signing key. The recipient verifies the signature with a trusted corresponding public key and the received message. Alteration makes verification fail under the scheme’s assumptions. Signatures support origin authentication, integrity, and evidence of authorization/non-repudiation, subject to key custody and identity trust. A valid signature proves a key authorized the signed content; it does not automatically prove the content is true or secret. The textbook’s encrypted-digest diagram is a teaching model, not a universal implementation of every signature algorithm.',
 'Alice signs an invoice. If an attacker changes the amount, Bob’s verification fails. If Bob instead trusts an attacker’s substitute public key, he has not authenticated Alice.',
 'Verify the received message and signature using the trusted sender public key','Decrypt the message with the sender public key to guarantee secrecy|Compare two untrusted plain hashes only|Use the recipient private key to identify the sender',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 120–122'],[18,'signature diagram'],[11,'transaction signatures']],
 ('A forged invoice is correctly signed by an attacker’s key labeled Alice. What check is missing?','A trusted binding between the verification key and Alice','A longer TCP timeout|A second copy of the forged digest|A public copy of Alice’s private key','The cryptographic check authenticates the signing key; the recipient must also know whose key it is.'))
 lesson(7,'Attacks on cryptography','How do ciphertext analysis, downgrade, weak algorithms, and bad implementation differ?',
 'A strong mathematical primitive can fail in a system that exposes its keys or allows weaker options.',
 'Ciphertext-only analysis looks for useful patterns without the plaintext. A downgrade pushes a protocol into weaker settings. Deprecated algorithms have known weaknesses or no longer provide an adequate margin. Misconfiguration or implementation mistakes can expose keys, misuse randomness, or apply a primitive incorrectly. Collision attacks seek two matching-digest inputs; preimage attacks seek input for a particular digest. The birthday bound for an ideal n-bit digest is roughly 2^(n/2), distinct from a roughly 2^n generic preimage search.',
 'A strong encryption algorithm does not help if its key is bundled in a public file. That failure concerns key handling rather than breaking the cipher mathematically.',
 'Distinguish attacks on patterns, protocol choices, primitives, and implementation','Every cryptographic incident means AES was mathematically broken|A downgrade always improves protection|Collision and preimage searches are identical',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 123–126'],[18,'algorithm and collision attacks']],
 ('An attacker forces a system to negotiate an older weaker protocol option. Which attack is illustrated?','Downgrade','Steganography|Risk acceptance|Merkle membership','The attacker changes which protection is selected rather than directly finding a private key.'))
 lesson(7,'File encryption full-disk encryption and hardware','How do file encryption, disk encryption, TPM, HSM, and self-encrypting drives differ?',
 'Choose what needs protection and where keys should be handled. Encryption hardware improves some boundaries, but it is not immune to every attack.',
 'File encryption protects selected files or folders. Full-disk or volume encryption protects stored contents against offline access when keys are unavailable. A self-encrypting drive performs encryption in its controller. A TPM supports platform-bound key protection and measured-state uses; it is not simply a chip that encrypts all files or always refuses every altered boot. An HSM is a dedicated cryptographic key/operation boundary often serving applications or multiple systems. Hardware and firmware still require trustworthy implementation, configuration, and access controls. Recovery keys and backups are necessary operational considerations.',
 'A company may use disk encryption for lost laptops and an HSM for server signing keys. Neither prevents all misuse by an already authenticated running application.',
 'Match protected data and key operations to the appropriate boundary','A TPM automatically encrypts every network packet|Hardware encryption has no possible vulnerabilities|File encryption always protects every temporary file on the disk',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 127–130'],[18,'software and hardware encryption']],
 ('An organization needs protected signing-key operations for a server application. Which category most directly fits?','Hardware Security Module','A DNS resolver|An ordinary packet checksum|A phishing toolbar','An HSM is designed as a protected cryptographic key and operation boundary.'))
 lesson(7,'Cryptography resource tradeoffs','Why do low power and low latency affect cryptographic design?',
 'Security work consumes energy, memory, and time. Small devices and fast-response systems must budget all of them.',
 'Low-power sensors have limited energy and processing resources. Low-latency services must return results promptly. Lightweight cryptography aims to provide appropriate security within constrained environments; it is not permission to invent a weak cipher. Hardware acceleration can shift performance tradeoffs, but key management and implementation still matter. Evaluate the required property, threat, resource budget, and operational life rather than assuming the algorithm with the largest number is automatically the best system design.',
 'A battery-powered sensor needs authenticated readings without exhausting its battery. The design must protect integrity while meeting its power and timing budget.',
 'Balance security requirements with energy, memory, processing, and response time','Constrained devices need no authentication|Low latency means unlimited delay|Hardware acceleration removes all key-management risk',[[3,'Ch. 3, pp. 107–108']],
 ('A sensor stops responding because cryptographic work consumes its available energy. What tradeoff was missed?','Resources versus required security and latency','Public-key ownership versus IP address|False positive versus Merkle proof|FTP active versus passive mode','The device must meet its security goal within the energy and processing budget.'))
 lesson(1,'Stuxnet and misleading visibility','What does the lecture’s Stuxnet example teach about targeted systems and monitoring?',
 'The lesson is the combination of several attack paths and misleading observations, not a list of old numerical claims.',
 'Lecture 1 describes Stuxnet as a worm targeting industrial systems, using multiple previously unknown vulnerabilities and removable-media entry, and altering equipment behavior while showing apparently normal readings. Use it to explain targeted cyber-physical impact, trusted-looking telemetry, and the limits of perimeter isolation. The lecture’s dates and impact estimates are historical course claims. For an exam scenario, connect removable-media policy, endpoint controls, segmentation, independent monitoring, and recovery.',
 'An industrial operator sees normal readings while physical equipment behaves abnormally. Independent evidence is needed before treating one display as complete visibility.',
 'Targeted malware can combine entry methods with deceptive monitoring','Air gaps make removable-media controls unnecessary|Every zero-day attack is Stuxnet|Normal displayed readings prove the machine is healthy',[[12,'Stuxnet and security-failure examples']],
 ('An isolated industrial network accepts unknown USB devices. What assumption should a defender challenge?','That lack of an Internet connection removes all entry paths','That equipment ever needs maintenance|That recorded measurements can be compared|That software has versions','Removable media can carry a threat across a boundary that blocks normal network access.'))
 lesson(2,'Choose the security device','Which control best fits an IP block, a website category policy, or unapproved execution?',
 'Begin with the requested action. Address filtering, website mediation, and local program execution are different enforcement points.',
 'Lecture 2’s cases select firewalls/router ACLs or prevention for malicious connection sources; a proxy is the strongest course answer for blocking website categories; application allowlisting restricts unapproved programs. A passive IDS can alert but cannot itself guarantee a block. Host controls add visibility into local events. A packet sensor must actually receive the relevant traffic to observe it. Combine controls where the business case requires more than one kind of enforcement.',
 'A school needs to restrict gambling sites and stop unknown executables. A web proxy addresses the browsing policy; application controls address execution.',
 'Choose enforcement by traffic attributes, application requests, or host execution','A passive IDS alone guarantees all blocks|PCAP alone prevents execution|A checksum enforces website categories',[[13,'device comparisons and case studies']],
 ('Employees keep running unapproved programs. Which is the best direct course control?','Application allowlisting','A larger flow-log archive|A different DNS font|An email signature','Allowlisting restricts execution to approved applications rather than merely recording network activity.'))
 lesson(2,'Detection tuning and business cost','How do signature detection, anomaly detection, and tuning affect decisions?',
 'Signatures recognize described patterns. Anomaly detection compares activity with an expected pattern. Both can be wrong.',
 'A detection signature may be a byte pattern, behavior rule, hash, or other feature; it is not always one unique hexadecimal string embedded in the file. Anomaly detection may notice new behavior but can flag unusual legitimate work. A false positive can overload analysts or interrupt service when prevention acts; a false negative can let an attack continue. Tuning uses representative traffic and investigation feedback. Redundancy, scalability, and business needs constrain inspection design.',
 'A large legitimate backup suddenly triggers an anomaly rule. The team investigates and adjusts the rule without disabling monitoring for all other traffic.',
 'Tune detection using evidence and business impact','Every anomaly is malicious|Every signature is guaranteed unique|A zero alert rate proves perfect security',[[13,'IDS/IPS antivirus and availability'],[4,'09/01–09/06']],
 ('After blocking every unusual connection, a company loses its backup service. What needs review?','False-positive cost and prevention-rule tuning','Whether encryption can be decrypted|Whether all logs should be deleted|Whether checksums should be shorter','A prevention action can harm availability when a benign but unusual activity matches the rule.'))
 lesson(3,'Defacement and advance-fee scams','How do changing a website and a 419 scam differ in mechanism and business harm?',
 'Defacement changes what a site displays. An advance-fee scam persuades a person to give money or information for a promised reward.',
 'A site defacement may exploit a software weakness or stolen administrative credentials to alter public content, creating reputational and integrity harm. Defenses include safe configuration, updates, strong access control, restricted publishing privileges, monitoring, and restoration. A 419 scam builds trust around a claimed inheritance, lottery, or similar payout and then requests fees or sensitive information. Use independent verification and approved reporting, not participation in the claimed transaction.',
 'A changed homepage announcing false claims is a defacement. A message offering a large reward if an employee first pays a handling fee is advance-fee fraud.',
 'Defacement attacks content integrity; advance-fee fraud manipulates trust','Both necessarily use the same database exploit|419 is a cryptographic hash|A defacement is always a harmless prank',[[14,'defacement and 419 examples'],[15,'review and phishing examples']],
 ('A page was restored after defacement, but the stolen administrator password was not changed. What remains?','The attacker may retain the original access path','All confidentiality risk is eliminated|A guaranteed hard fork|An automatically patched application','Restoring content addresses an effect; investigation must also remove the cause of unauthorized access.'))
 lesson(4,'Investigating an email without trusting it','What evidence should you compare when reviewing a suspicious message?',
 'Separate the message’s claims from evidence added by trusted receiving systems. A name, logo, or urgent story is easy to imitate.',
 'Review sender/display name, sending domain, reply and return paths, routing hops, time/time zones, recipients, subject, and attachment/link context. Authentication results from a trusted receiver provide useful evidence but need interpretation. A mismatch or failed check can be significant; a pass does not prove the message’s business request is safe. Use known contact details to verify unusual requests. Preserve/report the original message according to the organization’s process.',
 'A message names a real professor but asks students to sign in on a lookalike site. The familiar name does not establish that the link or request is legitimate.',
 'Compare trusted header evidence with claimed identity and request context','A university logo proves identity|A display name proves the domain|A clean-looking body replaces header review',[[15,'slides 16–22'],[7,'phishing example']],
 ('An email passes a sender-domain check but asks for an unusual payment. Is the payment safe on that fact alone?','No; independently verify the business request','Yes, because any authenticated domain is trustworthy|Yes, because the email has a logo|No, because all email is malware','A legitimate or compromised account can send an unsafe request. Domain evidence does not authorize the payment.'))
 lesson(4,'Fake antivirus and fake disk repair','Why do professional-looking warnings still need independent verification?',
 'Scareware sells fear. A convincing screen can invent an infection or disk failure to pressure installation or payment.',
 'Lecture 4 distinguishes fake antivirus/rogueware and fake disk-defragmenter products. Both invent problems and request money or installation; installed malware may interfere with real security software or capture data. A polished interface and large scan count are claims, not proof. Use trusted support and already-approved security tools. If someone installed the product, investigate the endpoint and account activity rather than merely closing the warning.',
 'A website reports hundreds of disk errors and demands a fee. A page cannot establish that claim simply by displaying a scan animation.',
 'Verify warnings using trusted tools and support before acting','A professional icon proves the product is legitimate|Every browser warning means the disk was scanned|Paying always removes the infection',[[15,'slides 23–30']],
 ('A fake cleaner was installed and then closed. Why might more response be needed?','It may have left malware, changed settings, or exposed credentials','Closing a window always removes its files|Only the website’s spelling matters|Payment information can never be collected by software','The visible warning can be only one part of a broader endpoint compromise.'))
 lesson(5,'Controlled hardening and patch validation','Why test small groups of hardening changes and verify the rollout?',
 'A control must improve security while preserving required work. Small, measured changes are easier to troubleshoot than a hundred unexplained changes.',
 'Apply an appropriate baseline, test representative systems, stage changes, schedule deployment where necessary, and verify success with rollback available. Remove unnecessary services and limit privileged access. A firewall can reduce exposure to a vulnerable service, but that does not repair the flaw or eliminate other attack paths. Legacy tools such as EMET, WSUS, or old scanner screenshots illustrate functions, not a requirement to install historical versions. The four discussion factors reduce distribution, coordination, analysis, and testing pressure.',
 'Five baseline changes are tested before rollout. A broken business application can then be traced to a small set of candidate changes.',
 'Test staged changes and confirm both security and required functionality','Apply every change without records|Blocked traffic proves all flaws are fixed|Snapshots guarantee no future outage',[[16,'slides 5–8, 12–28'],[1,'discussion 1']],
 ('A patch console says deployment started. What must still be checked?','Whether the target devices installed it successfully and function correctly','Whether the installer’s filename is long|Whether the textbook named the vendor|Whether all alerts should now be ignored','Initiating deployment is not evidence of successful installation or acceptable business operation.'))
 lesson(5,'NAC policy and endpoint controls','How do NAC, GPO, endpoint firewall, and HIPS contribute different protections?',
 'NAC decides whether a device meets conditions for access. GPO distributes settings. Endpoint firewall and HIPS enforce or observe activity on a host.',
 'Lecture 5 uses a traveling workstation to explain posture checks: patch status, antivirus definitions, and required policies can influence access. GPO helps distribute Windows-domain policy settings. An endpoint firewall restricts network communication to host services, while HIPS can respond to selected local behaviors or patterns. None eliminates the need to verify actual configuration, investigate alerts, or protect accounts. A domain user can still have excessive rights if configured incorrectly.',
 'A returning laptop is checked before receiving normal access. Its required firewall policy must also be present and effective after connection.',
 'Access posture, configuration distribution, and local enforcement complement each other','GPO automatically authenticates every external site|A domain account can never be privileged|NAC replaces all patch deployment',[[16,'slides 17–22, 29'],[4,'09/15']],
 ('A business wants required workstation settings centrally applied. Which course mechanism best fits?','Group Policy Objects','A packet checksum|A Merkle tree|ROT13','GPO distributes domain policy; NAC can check posture but is not the same distribution mechanism.'))
 lesson(6,'Manual automated static and dynamic','Why are these four analysis labels not interchangeable?',
 'Manual versus automated asks who performs the work. Static versus dynamic asks whether the code being analyzed runs.',
 'A person can read source statically or debug running code dynamically. A tool can automate either approach. Lecture 6 names Firebug and Malzilla for manual review and JSUnpack, Wepawet, and reputation services under automated tools. Inspect source for suspicious redirects, embedded frames, scripts, form handling, and external references. Legitimate pages can contain those features too, so context and behavior matter. JSUnpack is the likely sample answer but the slide’s actual category is automated, not a definitive purely-static classification.',
 'A script automatically scans source without executing it: automated/static. A human watches an isolated program’s network changes: manual/dynamic.',
 'Manual/automated and static/dynamic describe two independent dimensions','All automated analysis is static|All manual analysis executes code|Every iframe proves malware',[[17,'slides 6–17'],[1,'MC 5']],
 ('A tool executes suspicious JavaScript in an isolated environment and records redirects. Which labels fit?','Automated and dynamic','Manual and static|Automated and necessarily static|Reputation-only and manual','Execution makes the analysis dynamic; software performing it makes it automated.'))
 lesson(6,'Reputation and behavior evidence','Why can two website scanners disagree, and why does time matter?',
 'A site can change after its last scan. A reputation label summarizes previous observations, not every possible current response.',
 'Reputation services rely on their scan history, reporting, and classification. A newly compromised legitimate page may not yet be listed. Different user agents, times, or visitor conditions may receive different content. Dynamic inspection observes files, registry, processes, and connections in a controlled environment; a negative result may reflect the test conditions. Isolation reduces risk but is not an absolute guarantee. Do not browse historical malicious domains from lecture screenshots as study exercises.',
 'One service reports an old clean scan, while a sandbox sees a new redirect only for a particular browser. The evidence needs reconciliation, not blind voting.',
 'Compare observation time, test conditions, and the evidence behind each result','A recognizable URL guarantees safety forever|One clean reputation score proves no compromise|A VM can never affect its host',[[17,'slides 3–5, 15–28']],
 ('Yesterday’s clean reputation result conflicts with today’s suspicious redirect. Which is the best interpretation?','The page or served content may have changed; investigate current evidence','The redirect must be harmless|Reputation tools are always correct|Every browser must receive identical content','Reputation depends on when and how observations were made.'))
 lesson(7,'Merkle proofs and hash links','How does a Merkle proof differ from a link to the previous block?',
 'One commitment summarizes transactions inside a block. Another connects that block to earlier history.',
 'A Merkle tree combines transaction hashes into a root. A membership proof supplies enough sibling hashes to recompute the trusted root without downloading every transaction. It shows inclusion under that root, not that a real-world claim is true or a spend valid. A previous-block hash commits to the preceding header. Non-membership proofs require appropriate ordered/authenticated structures; not every ordinary Merkle tree automatically proves absence. Changing data affects its commitment and descendant links.',
 'A lightweight client can verify inclusion using a transaction and its proof path, but still needs an appropriately trusted chain/header view and validation assumptions.',
 'Merkle proofs show inclusion under a root; prior hashes link block history','A Merkle proof reveals every owner’s private key|Membership proves every business claim is true|Any unsorted tree automatically proves absence',[[18,'block components and Merkle-tree topics'],[11,'blockchain questions']],
 ('A transaction’s hash recomputes to a known Merkle root using a proof path. What has been shown?','Inclusion under that committed root','That the transaction’s real-world description is true|That the owner’s private key is public|That all competing chains disappeared','A commitment proves a data relationship. Other validation and consensus checks establish different properties.'))
 lesson(7,'Consensus double spending and forks','Why do valid signatures alone not solve the double-spending problem?',
 'The same owner might authorize two conflicting spends. The network needs rules and agreement about which valid history to accept.',
 'Nodes independently validate transactions and blocks. Bitcoin’s proof-of-work chain selection follows greatest cumulative work among valid chains; the lecture’s longest-chain phrase is a simplification. The Byzantine-generals theme concerns agreement despite faulty or malicious participants. Confirmations reduce reorganization risk under the security assumptions. A hard fork changes rules incompatibly; a soft fork restricts accepted rules compatibly for older nodes. Neither term simply means every competing branch survives or disappears by natural selection.',
 'Two transactions spend the same previous output and both have genuine owner signatures. They cannot both enter the same valid transaction history.',
 'Validation and consensus choose a valid history among conflicting possibilities','A valid signature makes every conflicting spend acceptable|Any chain with more block count is always valid|Every hard fork guarantees two permanent chains',[[18,'security challenges chain selection and forks'],[11,'Nakamoto exercise']],
 ('A longer-looking chain contains an invalid spend. Should nodes accept it just because it has more blocks?','No; validity is checked before applying chain-selection rules','Yes, block count overrides every rule|Yes, if the sender signed twice|Only if a checksum matches','Proof of work or chain length does not authorize violating transaction or consensus rules.'))
 lesson(8,'Packets encapsulation and headers','How do application data, TCP segments, and IP packets fit together?',
 'Picture nested envelopes. Each layer adds information for its own job; the whole packet is not just the user’s message.',
 'Application data can be carried in a TCP segment with ports, sequence/acknowledgment fields, and a checksum. IP wraps transport data with addresses and routing-related header fields. A link-layer frame carries the IP packet on a particular link. Packet lengths vary within protocol/link constraints. TCP is transport/OSI layer 4; IP is network/OSI layer 3. TCP does not choose the Internet route or provide encryption. Checksum error detection is distinct from authenticated integrity.',
 'A short message gains TCP and IP headers before link transmission. A capture can show ports and addresses even when higher-level contents are encrypted.',
 'Transport data is encapsulated inside IP, then a link frame','TCP contains the entire Internet routing table|Every IP packet has the same fixed size|IP automatically encrypts the payload',[[19,'packet/header figures and TCP/IP overview']],
 ('Ignoring fragmentation, an IP packet carries a TCP segment plus an IP header. Which is larger?','The IP packet because it includes the TCP segment and its own header','The TCP segment because it includes the IP header|They must always have the same length|Neither can contain application data','Encapsulation adds an outer header. The lecture’s packet-size question tests that nesting relationship.'))
 lesson(8,'Firewall types and return-traffic assumptions','How do packet filtering, circuit gateways, and application gateways differ?',
 'Different controls see different details. A port or flag alone does not prove a request belongs to a permitted conversation.',
 'The lecture diagram associates packet filtering with network-level checks, circuit gateways with connection/session establishment, application gateways with higher-level mediation, and MAC-layer controls with link identifiers. Modern products may combine capabilities. Stateful filtering tracks connection context; a simplistic rule allowing anything resembling return traffic can be misleading. A proxy may authenticate users and restrict requests, but its visibility and policies determine effectiveness. Compare business goals and inspectable evidence rather than assuming one name guarantees protection.',
 'A network rule sees an IP and port; an application proxy can apply a website-category policy. Neither replaces endpoint evidence about which process initiated a connection.',
 'Inspect information available at the relevant layer and connection context','Every allowed ACK proves an established session|A proxy always defeats every reverse shell|MAC filtering verifies a person’s identity',[[19,'firewall/OSI and SYN-ACK scanning figures'],[13,'firewall and proxy comparisons']],
 ('A rule accepts any packet with ACK set as trusted return traffic without checking connection state. What is the weak assumption?','A flag alone proves an authorized existing connection','IP addresses can never be spoofed|TCP never uses flags|Application gateways cannot log requests','A packet can claim a flag pattern; a stateful decision needs actual connection context.'))
 lesson(8,'Secure protocol choices','How do FTP, FTPS, SFTP, HTTPS, S/MIME, SRTP, and NTP differ?',
 'Similar names can hide different protocols. Choose the protection and application first.',
 'Traditional FTP uses separate control/data connections without encryption. FTPS adds TLS to FTP; require the intended protection for control and data rather than assuming both are protected. SFTP is file transfer over SSH, not FTP with a secure label. HTTPS protects HTTP with TLS. S/MIME supports email content signing/encryption, subject to key and client support. SRTP protects real-time media. NTP synchronizes time; plain NTP alone is not a cryptographic guarantee (NTS is a separate security mechanism). DNSSEC authenticates DNS data and does not encrypt ordinary queries. These clarify the lecture’s simplified secure-protocol table.',
 'A company moving files chooses SFTP over SSH. A signed email may remain signed beyond one transport connection, unlike merely protecting its delivery link with TLS.',
 'Match the application and verify the actual security mechanism','SFTP and FTPS are identical|Plain NTP guarantees authenticated time|DNSSEC encrypts every web connection',[[19,'secure file transfer email and protocol table']],
 ('A server protects only the FTP control connection with TLS. Are all transferred file contents necessarily protected?','No; data-channel protection must also be configured and required','Yes, the letters FTPS guarantee every channel|Yes, because FTP has a checksum|No, because TLS can never protect FTP','FTP separates control and data. Securing one channel does not by itself establish the other’s protection.'))
 lesson(8,'DNS SNMP and diagnostic evidence','What can DNS, SNMP, and ICMP tell an administrator?',
 'Naming, management statistics, and reachability are different questions. A single successful diagnostic does not prove the whole service is healthy.',
 'DNS provides hierarchical distributed name records. Poisoning can redirect resolution; validated DNSSEC checks signed DNS data. Restricting unintended zone transfer reduces information exposure. SNMP agents expose permitted management information; versions 1/2c use community strings, while v3 supports stronger authentication/privacy depending on configuration. ICMP echo can test reachability and latency, but a blocked response does not prove the host is down. A successful ping does not prove that an application works.',
 'A device answers ping but its web application fails. The network evidence establishes only part of the service path, so application logs and connection tests are needed.',
 'Use each protocol’s evidence for the question it can answer','A successful ping proves every application is healthy|SNMP community strings always encrypt traffic|DNSSEC makes all returned websites trustworthy',[[19,'SNMP ICMP and DNS topics']],
 ('A host does not answer ping, but its website works. Which explanation is plausible?','ICMP is filtered while the web service remains reachable','The host has no IP address|The website must be a hash collision|TCP requires every ping to succeed','Diagnostic traffic can be controlled independently from application traffic.'))
 lesson(8,'Port mirrors taps and sensor placement','Why does sensor placement determine what defenders can see?',
 'A sensor cannot inspect traffic it never receives. Define the visibility question before selecting its network location.',
 'A switch mirror copies selected port traffic to a monitoring port. A network tap provides a traffic copy from a link. Both have operational limitations; traffic may be dropped, selected incompletely, or remain encrypted. Sensors detect, collectors gather, filters enforce, and correlation engines combine evidence. Placement should cover relevant boundaries, internal movement, and business assets; simply choosing the busiest link can miss a smaller critical segment. TLS termination changes which content may be visible.',
 'A gateway sensor sees Internet traffic but not two servers talking locally on another switch. An internal mirror or other telemetry may be needed.',
 'Place sensors where the required traffic is observable and correlate other evidence','The busiest link reveals every network action|Mirroring automatically decrypts TLS|A collector always blocks malware inline',[[19,'mirror/tap figures and placement topics'],[13,'visibility']],
 ('An analyst has no records of traffic between two internal servers. What should be checked first?','Whether telemetry covers the path between those servers','Whether every disk is encrypted|Whether the public homepage has a logo|Whether the hash has 256 characters','Missing evidence may be a visibility gap rather than proof the communication did not occur.'))
 lesson(8,'Logs correlation and retention','How do access audit event and security logs support an investigation?',
 'Each log is a partial witness. Put the witnesses on a common timeline and preserve the evidence needed to explain what happened.',
 'Access logs show resource requests, audit logs record accountable actions, event logs record system/application events, and security logs describe security-related observations. Exact fields vary. Correlate identity, host, and network events using reliable time and context. Watch rejected traffic, unusual outbound connections, unsuccessful logins, and unexpected file changes. Centralized analysis helps handle volume and format differences, but collection gaps, clock differences, retention limits, and tampering affect conclusions. File-integrity monitoring detects changes; an alert still needs interpretation.',
 'An unusual login precedes a new process and outbound transfer. Together these support a stronger timeline than any one record alone.',
 'Correlate partial records while checking time, coverage, integrity, and retention','One log contains every action forever|No retained event proves nothing happened|All file changes are definitely malicious',[[19,'analyzing security data and log management'],[11,'visibility exercise']],
 ('Two systems show different times for related events. What should an analyst account for?','Clock synchronization, time zones, and timestamp meaning','Only the alphabetical order of usernames|Whether ciphertext is readable|The number of worksheet columns','Timeline comparisons are unreliable until differences in clock and logging conventions are understood.'))
 lesson(8,'Hypervisors containers and virtual desktops','How do Type I Type II containers VDI and VM sprawl differ?',
 'A VM has a guest operating system. A container usually shares the host kernel. A virtual desktop describes where a user’s desktop runs.',
 'Type I hypervisors run directly on hardware; Type II hypervisors run above a host OS, as in the VirtualBox lab. Containers package applications and dependencies while sharing a kernel, with different isolation boundaries from VMs. VDI hosts user desktops on servers. Snapshots and live migration support operations but do not automatically remove downtime or replace backups. VM escape is a possible isolation failure; VM sprawl is unmanaged growth. Protect the hypervisor, guests, management plane, and virtual networks.',
 'An organization creates twenty test VMs and forgets half of them. Those systems still need ownership, updates, and monitoring.',
 'Distinguish the OS and management boundaries of each model','Every container has its own full guest kernel|A snapshot is an independent backup by definition|VMs never need security updates',[[19,'virtualization figures and advantages/risks'],[9,'VirtualBox lab']],
 ('A hypervisor is installed as an application on a laptop’s operating system. Which type fits?','Type II','Type I|A Merkle tree|A DNS authoritative server','Type II depends on a host OS; Type I runs on the hardware boundary.'))
 lesson(8,'Cloud models and responsibility','How do SaaS PaaS IaaS and cloud deployment models affect security duties?',
 'The provider manages some layers and the customer manages others. Renting infrastructure does not transfer every security decision.',
 'SaaS supplies an application; PaaS supplies a managed platform for customer applications; IaaS supplies infrastructure where customers manage their OS/applications. SECaaS delivers security services. Public/private/community/hybrid describe deployment arrangements, not the same categories as service models. Protect identities, configurations, data, transmission, tenant isolation, and availability according to responsibility. A CASB helps extend enterprise policy to cloud usage. Distributed processing does not by itself mean every participant has equal authority.',
 'An IaaS customer still patches its own guest OS. A SaaS customer may not patch the vendor’s servers but still controls its own user access and data-sharing choices.',
 'Allocate duties by service model and verify the actual shared-responsibility boundary','Cloud always means the provider patches every customer OS|Private cloud means there is no network|SaaS and hybrid are the same kind of category',[[19,'cloud models and security challenges'],[16,'patch coordination']],
 ('A company runs its own OS on rented virtual infrastructure. Who normally manages that guest OS’s updates?','The customer under the IaaS responsibility arrangement','Nobody because cloud servers are immune|Every other tenant|Only the public DNS operator','Infrastructure rental does not automatically transfer guest-OS administration.'))
 
