 for term,meaning,num,alternatives in [
 ('Exploit','A technique or code that takes advantage of a vulnerability.',47,'The weakness itself|The business asset’s value|A cryptographic digest'),
 ('Payload','The action or code delivered or executed after an attack reaches its target.',31,'The public verification key|Only a firewall rule|An assurance that a page is safe'),
 ('Sandbox','An isolated environment for observing a program or file under controlled conditions.',74,'The same thing as the PowerShell language|A guarantee no escape is possible|An email routing header'),
 ('PowerShell','An administration shell and scripting environment that may be used legitimately or misused.',34,'A malware family by definition|A network checksum|A hardware-only keylogger'),
 ('Network reconnaissance','Gathering information about systems, services, and exposure to understand a target.',34,'Recovering plaintext from ciphertext|Proving a transaction belongs to a Merkle tree|Automatically patching every host'),
 ('Credential dumping','Extracting stored authentication material from a system.',34,'Changing a DNS record|Signing a legitimate payment|Listing only public website titles'),
 ('Privilege escalation','Gaining higher rights than the initial access allowed.',34,'Making a program’s window larger|Encrypting a file with a public key|Sending an ordinary DNS query'),
 ('Process hollowing','A technique that places malicious code within the context of another process to conceal or execute it.',34,'Removing empty rows from a log|Every ordinary application launch|A firewall category list'),
 ('Infiltration','Gaining an unauthorized presence inside a system or network.',34,'Moving information out of the organization|A standard integrity checksum|Restoring a known-good snapshot'),
 ('Exfiltration','Moving information from a system to an unauthorized destination.',34,'Only opening a local terminal|Installing an approved patch|Public-key verification'),
 ('TPM','A platform-oriented component supporting protected keys and measured-state uses.',63,'A chip that automatically encrypts every network packet|A guarantee against every running malware process|A replacement for all backup copies'),
 ('HSM','A dedicated boundary for protected cryptographic keys and operations.',63,'A passive network traffic summary|The same thing as every laptop BIOS|A tool for generating fake login pages'),
 ('CASB','A cloud access security broker helps apply enterprise policy to cloud usage.',84,'A universal replacement for application authentication|A Bitcoin signature scheme|An IP checksum'),
 ('Hypervisor','Software that manages virtual machines and their access to underlying resources.',83,'A public-key certificate|A malware signature database only|A network naming hierarchy')]:
  q=Q[num-1];D['vocab'].append(dict(id=f'425-extra-term-{len(D["vocab"])+1}',source=num,section=q['section'],term=term,exp='',q=f'What does {term} mean?',correct=meaning,answer=meaning,teach=meaning+' '+q['teach'],example=q['example'],distractors=alternatives.split('|')))
 for source,prompt,correct,wrong,why in [
 (22,'A grade is changed without permission. Which CIA goal is most directly violated?','Integrity','Confidentiality only|Availability only|Vendor diversity','The central problem is an unauthorized alteration, even if nobody copied the record or interrupted access.'),
 (22,'An outsider reads a private medical record without changing it. Which CIA goal is most directly violated?','Confidentiality','Availability only|Integrity only|Simplicity','Unauthorized disclosure is the key fact; alteration and downtime are not required.'),
 (22,'A portal cannot serve legitimate students during an attack. Which CIA goal is directly affected?','Availability','Obfuscation|Non-repudiation|Key exchange','Authorized users cannot access the needed service.'),
 (48,'A business stops offering a service because its risk is unacceptable. Which response fits?','Avoidance','Transfer|Mitigation while continuing|Acceptance','Avoidance means deciding not to undertake the activity, rather than only adding controls.'),
 (48,'A business buys insurance against some breach-related loss. Which response fits?','Transfer of some financial consequences','Repair of all vulnerabilities|Removal of every responsibility|Avoidance of all activity','Insurance may transfer covered losses but does not patch systems or remove every operational duty.'),
 (17,'Which malware category is defined by unsolicited advertising behavior?','Adware','Worm|Backdoor|Macro virus','Adware describes advertising behavior; it does not uniquely specify a propagation mechanism.'),
 (17,'A routine attached to an executable activates when the executable runs. Which virus category fits?','Program or file-infecting virus','Network worm by definition|Hardware keylogger|DNS poisoning','The host executable carries the virus. A worm is defined by independent propagation.'),
 (17,'A malicious document macro runs after the user enables it. Which virus category fits?','Macro virus','Standalone network worm|A packet checksum|A BIOS firewall','The malicious instructions are associated with the document’s macro mechanism.'),
 (17,'A virus chooses among a limited set of predefined mutations. Which term fits the textbook?','Oligomorphic','Metamorphic|A normal TCP handshake|A network tap','Oligomorphic uses a finite predefined collection; metamorphic rewriting produces equivalent code structures.'),
 (17,'A virus rewrites its own code into a logically equivalent form. Which term fits?','Metamorphic','Oligomorphic only|A macro by definition|A checksum','Metamorphic describes rewriting the code while keeping equivalent behavior.'),
 (17,'Malicious code is distributed through pieces of an infected program, with encrypted code and a split decoder. Which course technique is illustrated?','Swiss cheese infection','A TLS handshake|A firewall state table|A valid Merkle proof','The textbook describes spreading the decryption engine through the host code to hinder detection.'),
 (17,'A program quietly sends a user’s browsing activity to another party. Which behavior category fits?','Spyware','A firewall|A reliability protocol|A security baseline','The defining behavior is covert collection, not necessarily how the program arrived.'),
 (17,'A concealed access method bypasses the usual login checks. What is it?','Backdoor','Checksum|A normal public key|A false positive','A backdoor concerns unauthorized access; it can coexist with other malware capabilities.'),
 (28,'A SYN flood strains connection state. Why might increasing disk space alone not fix it?','The exhausted resource is connection handling, not necessarily disk storage','SYN floods only affect file names|TCP has no connection state|Every DoS is a hash collision','Mitigation must address the actual bottleneck rather than adding an unrelated resource.'),
 (29,'Which control directly keeps user-supplied values out of SQL command structure?','Parameterized queries','HTML output encoding alone|A longer cookie lifetime|A packet checksum','Binding values as data prevents those values from becoming SQL syntax; output encoding addresses another interpreter.'),
 (29,'A comment must appear as plain text in a page. Which defense directly prevents interpreting it as executable markup?','Context-appropriate output encoding or a safe text API','A prepared SQL statement alone|A larger TCP window|Hiding the database product name','The browser is the interpreter at this point. Protect the output context rather than only the database query.'),
 (29,'Why is WebGoat intentionally vulnerable?','To provide a controlled teaching environment for understanding and fixing application flaws','To recommend insecure production defaults|To serve as an antivirus scanner|To guarantee every web application is safe','The purpose is learning in an authorized training environment.'),
 (31,'Fake test credentials appear in the lab attacker’s terminal after form submission. What does that demonstrate directly?','The submitted form data reached the credential-harvesting endpoint','That every file on the victim was stolen|That the victim OS was necessarily exploited|That MFA was mathematically broken','The evidence supports credential collection. It does not prove unrelated endpoint execution or full compromise.'),
 (32,'A hardware keylogger sits between a keyboard and computer. Why can patching alone be insufficient?','The capture path can be physical hardware rather than a software flaw','Patches always disable USB devices|Only network packets can be recorded|Hardware can never capture passwords','Physical inspection and access control address a different boundary from software patching.'),
 (34,'An attacker moves from a user account to higher administrative rights. What has occurred?','Privilege escalation','Only reconnaissance|Hash collision|Data-at-rest encryption','The defining change is increased authority, which may enable additional actions.'),
 (34,'A large outbound transfer follows suspicious credential access. What should an analyst conclude immediately?','It warrants correlation and investigation; the records alone may not prove exactly what was stolen','Every company file was definitely stolen|The transfer must be legitimate|All encryption was broken','Separate observed events from hypotheses about their content and impact.'),
 (38,'Alice wants only Bob to recover a message using public-key encryption. Which key does Alice use?','Bob’s public encryption key','Alice’s public key|Bob’s private key copied to Alice|A public checksum','Confidentiality for a recipient uses the recipient’s public encryption capability and their private decryption capability.'),
 (18,'Alice signs a message but does not encrypt its contents. Can an observer read the message?','Yes, if the message is otherwise sent in readable form','No, all signatures encrypt the entire message|Only if the observer has Alice’s private key|No, hashing hides every original message','A signature protects authenticity/integrity under trust assumptions; it does not inherently provide secrecy.'),
 (19,'A digest has 256 bits. How many hexadecimal characters are needed to display it?','64','32|128|256','Each hexadecimal character represents four bits: 256 divided by 4 equals 64.'),
 (19,'An ideal 256-bit hash has a generic birthday collision-search scale near which value?','2^128','2^256 for any pair|256 attempts|64 attempts','Searching for any matching pair benefits from birthday reasoning. Finding an input for a specific target is a different problem.'),
 (21,'Does a valid Bitcoin signature alone prove an output has not already been spent?','No; transaction validation must also check the unspent state','Yes, signatures automatically order all transactions|Yes, the receiver’s address encrypts time|No, Bitcoin uses no signatures','Authorization and double-spend validation solve different problems.'),
 (40,'A miner changes the nonce and recomputes the block-header hash. What is the goal?','Find a hash satisfying the proof-of-work target','Decrypt a private key|Guarantee anonymity|Increase the digest’s length','Mining searches candidate headers; verification checks the resulting hash against the target.'),
 (41,'Why are block subsidy and transaction fees relevant to Bitcoin’s design?','They incentivize participation in block production','They let miners spend any user’s output|They encrypt addresses|They guarantee every miner is honest','Incentives support the system’s economic model but are not blanket proof that attacks cannot happen.'),
 (76,'Why can additional confirmations reduce the risk of a Bitcoin history rewrite?','Rewriting accepted history requires overcoming more accumulated work under the security assumptions','More confirmations reveal every private key|They eliminate all possible collisions|They make signatures unnecessary','The security argument depends on validation, competing work, and network assumptions, not absolute immutability.'),
 (42,'A real-time voice app values timely audio over retransmitting old audio. Which transport tradeoff is relevant?','UDP can support datagrams without TCP-style delivery guarantees','UDP is permitted only on private networks|TCP guarantees zero delay|IP guarantees application-level delivery','Applications may choose how to handle loss and latency; neither choice itself supplies encryption.'),
 (15,'An attacker changes a packet and recomputes its ordinary checksum. Why might the receiver accept the checksum?','The checksum has no secret that prevents that recomputation','The checksum authenticates the sender’s identity|TCP encrypts the checksum|All packet contents are immutable','Error-detection checks are not the same as cryptographic authentication.'),
 (43,'Why can passive FTP behave differently through a firewall than active FTP?','It changes how the separate data connection is established','It removes all data connections|It encrypts every password automatically|It changes FTP into SFTP','The control and data paths are distinct; the negotiated passive data port affects connection handling.'),
 (81,'A mirror receives only selected switch ports. Can it prove no malicious traffic existed elsewhere?','No; its coverage is limited to the traffic actually copied','Yes, because mirrors see every network globally|Yes, if it has a large disk|No, because all mirrored traffic is encrypted','Visibility boundaries constrain what an absence of observed traffic can mean.'),
 (83,'Why is a VM snapshot not automatically an independent backup?','It may depend on the original virtual disk and shared underlying storage','Snapshots cannot preserve state|All backups require Bitcoin|A snapshot always duplicates the entire physical server','A failure of the shared storage or base image can affect both the VM and dependent snapshots.'),
 (84,'A provider supplies a managed platform on which a customer deploys its own application. Which service model fits?','PaaS','SaaS only|A private-cloud deployment category|ICMP','Platform as a Service supplies the application platform; the customer still has application and data responsibilities.')]:
  q=Q[source-1];D['checks'].append(dict(id=f'425-focus-{len(D["checks"])+1}',source=source,section=q['section'],q=prompt,correct=correct,distractors=wrong.split('|'),teach=why,answer=why,example=q['example'],format='mc'))
 D['counts']={k:len(D[k]) for k in ['questions','checks','vocab','cloze','sorts','trueFalse','sources']}
